🔄 What is a Circular Reference in Java/Spring Boot?
A circular reference happens when two objects reference each other in a loop — this becomes problematic especially during JSON serialization.

@Entity
public class Post {
    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "post", cascade = CascadeType.ALL)
    private List<Comment> comments;
}

@Entity
public class Comment {
    @ManyToOne
    @JoinColumn(name = "post_id")
    private Post post;
}

🔁 When Returned as JSON:
When you return a Post, it contains comments, and each Comment contains the post again, and again...
Post {
  comments: [
    { post: { comments: [...] } }
  ]
}
This leads to:
❌ StackOverflowError
❌ "Infinite recursion" error in Postman / console logs


🔄 Relationship Summary

| Relationship  | Example (A → B)      | Cardinality | Use When...                  |
| ------------- | -------------------- | ----------- | ---------------------------- |
| `@OneToOne`   | User → Profile       | 1 to 1      | One item linked to one other |
| `@OneToMany`  | Post → List<Comment> | 1 to many   | One item to many             |
| `@ManyToOne`  | Comment → Post       | many to 1   | Many items belong to one     |
| `@ManyToMany` | Student ↔ Course     | many ↔ many | Students take many courses   |

Default Values by Relationship:

| Relationship  | Default Fetch Type |
| ------------- | ------------------ |
| `@OneToMany`  | LAZY               |
| `@ManyToMany` | LAZY               |
| `@ManyToOne`  | EAGER              |
| `@OneToOne`   | EAGER              |


🔷 3. FetchType (LAZY vs EAGER)
| FetchType | Meaning                                                          |
| --------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `EAGER`   | Loads child entities **immediately** with the parent             |
| `LAZY`    | Loads child **only when accessed** (recommended for collections) |


